-- title:
--   Dynamic flows (9.2)–(9.5), chain decompositions, temporally repeated flows and dynamic cuts in $G(p)$
-- statement:
--   Following Chapter III, §9 of Ford and Fulkerson's *Flows in Networks*, this file defines dynamic flows over $p$ periods and the objects of the maximal dynamic flow theorem. The capacity $c(x, y)$ and the traversal time $a(x, y)$ of each arc are as in the network file.
--
--   1. A **dynamic flow from $s$ to $t$ for $p$ periods with value $v(p)$** consists of amounts $f(x, y; \tau) \ge 0$, the flow leaving $x$ along the arc $(x, y)$ at time $\tau$ and arriving at $y$ at time $\tau + a(x, y)$, and **hold-overs** $f(x, x; \tau) \ge 0$ at $x$ from $\tau$ to $\tau + 1$ (traversal time $1$, unbounded capacity), for $\tau = 0, 1, \dots, p$, satisfying
--   $$\text{(9.2)}\ \sum_{\tau=0}^{p} \sum_{y \in N} [f(s, y; \tau) - f(y, s; \tau - a(y, s))] - v(p) = 0,$$
--   $$\text{(9.3)}\ \sum_{y \in N} [f(x, y; \tau) - f(y, x; \tau - a(y, x))] = 0 \quad (x \neq s, t;\ \tau = 0, 1, \dots, p),$$
--   $$\text{(9.4)}\ \sum_{\tau=0}^{p} \sum_{y \in N} [f(t, y; \tau) - f(y, t; \tau - a(y, t))] + v(p) = 0,$$
--   $$\text{(9.5)}\ 0 \le f(x, y; \tau) \le c(x, y),$$
--   where a variable with a negative time index, or for a pair $(x, y)$, $x \ne y$, that is not an arc, is absent. It is **maximal** if no dynamic flow for $p$ periods has a larger value; $\bar v(p)$ denotes the maximal value.
--   2. A **chain decomposition** of a flow $f$ of value $v$ is a finite family of **chain flows** $(C_r; h_r)$: chains $C_r$ from $s$ to $t$ and amounts $h_r > 0$, with $\sum_{r:\ (x, y) \in C_r} h_r \le f(x, y)$ on every arc and $\sum_r h_r \ge v$.
--   3. The **temporally repeated flow** generated by the chain flows $(C_r; h_r)$ for $p$ periods starts each chain flow at every time $\theta = 0, 1, \dots$ with $\theta + \sigma_r \le p$, where $\sigma_r$ is the traversal time of $C_r$ (the sum of $a$ over its arcs); the copy started at $\theta$ sends $h_r$ into the $i$-th arc of $C_r$ at time $\theta + \tau_i$, with $\tau_1 = 0$ and $\tau_{i+1} = \tau_i + a(x_i, x_{i+1})$. It uses no hold-overs.
--   4. The **time-expanded network** $G(p)$ has nodes $x(\tau)$, $\tau = 0, \dots, p$, replica arcs $[x(\tau), y(\tau + a(x, y))]$ of capacity $c(x, y)$ for $0 \le \tau \le p - a(x, y)$, and hold-over arcs $[x(\tau), x(\tau + 1)]$ of infinite capacity. A **dynamic cut** is given by a node set $X$ of $G(p)$ containing every source $s(\tau)$ and no sink $t(\tau)$; the cut is the set of arcs from $X$ to its complement, and its capacity is the sum of their capacities. A **minimal dynamic cut** is a dynamic cut of least capacity.
--   5. For integer node numbers $\pi$, (9.18) $X = \{x(\tau) \mid \pi(x) \le \tau\}$.
--
--   **Formalization Note** Time indices are natural numbers. `f x y τ` and `hold x τ` are separate functions; hold-overs are only required to be nonnegative (infinite capacity is never written as a number). The absent variables are encoded by summing over arcs only and by guarding the incoming terms with $a(y, x) \le \tau$ (and $1 \le \tau$ for hold-overs), so no truncated subtraction occurs. Values at times $\tau > p$ occur in no constraint. A node $x(\tau)$ of $G(p)$ is the pair $(x, \tau)$ and a node set is a `Finset (N × ℕ)`, pairs with $\tau > p$ being ignored. A cut crossed by a hold-over arc has infinite capacity; the capacity function counts replica arcs only and a minimal dynamic cut is required to have no crossing hold-over arc and capacity at most that of every other such cut, which is equivalent. A chain decomposition is any family with the properties of Chapter I, Theorem 2.2 rather than the particular greedy family of (2.9)–(2.10), since "any decomposition will serve" (p. 88).
-- source:
--   Ford & Fulkerson, Flows in Networks, RAND Report R-375-PR (1962), Chapter III, pp. 81–83 (§9, (9.1)–(9.5), the network G(p)), pp. 84–88 (temporally repeated flows, (9.6)–(9.11)), p. 89 ((9.17)–(9.18)); Chapter I, pp. 13–15 (§2, chain decomposition, Theorem 2.2)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_FlowsNetworks_DynamicFlow_Network

namespace FlowsNetworks.DynamicFlow

variable {N : Type} [Fintype N] [DecidableEq N]

/-- The amount "leaving" node `x` at time `τ` in a dynamic flow (Chapter III, §9, p. 81):
`∑_y f(x, y; τ)` over the arcs `(x, y) ∈ 𝒜`, plus the hold-over `f(x, x; τ)` from `τ` to `τ + 1`,
here the separate variable `hold x τ`. -/
def outAt (G : Network N) (f : N → N → ℕ → ℝ) (hold : N → ℕ → ℝ) (x : N) (τ : ℕ) : ℝ :=
  (∑ q ∈ G.arcs.filter (fun q => q.1 = x), f q.1 q.2 τ) + hold x τ

/-- The amount "entering" node `x` at time `τ` (p. 81): `∑_y f(y, x; τ − a(y, x))` over the arcs
`(y, x) ∈ 𝒜`, plus the hold-over `f(x, x; τ − 1)` (hold-overs have `a(x, x) = 1`). A variable with
a negative time index is suppressed (p. 82): the term for `(y, x)` is present only when
`a(y, x) ≤ τ`, and the hold-over term only when `1 ≤ τ`. -/
def inAt (G : Network N) (f : N → N → ℕ → ℝ) (hold : N → ℕ → ℝ) (x : N) (τ : ℕ) : ℝ :=
  (∑ q ∈ G.arcs.filter (fun q => q.2 = x ∧ G.cost q.1 q.2 ≤ τ),
      f q.1 q.2 (τ - G.cost q.1 q.2)) +
    (if 1 ≤ τ then hold x (τ - 1) else 0)

/-- A dynamic flow from `s` to `t` for `p` periods with value `v` (Chapter III, §9, (9.2)–(9.5),
pp. 81–82). `f x y τ` is the amount `f(x, y; τ)` leaving `x` along the arc `(x, y)` at time `τ`
and arriving at `y` at time `τ + a(x, y)`; `hold x τ` is the hold-over `f(x, x; τ)` at `x` from `τ`
to `τ + 1` (traversal time `a(x, x) = 1`, capacity `c(x, x) = ∞`, so it is only required to be
nonnegative). Departure times are `τ = 0, 1, …, p`; values of `f` and `hold` at times `τ > p`
and of `f` off `𝒜` occur in none of the constraints.

* (9.2) `∑_{τ=0}^{p} ∑_{y ∈ N} [f(s, y; τ) − f(y, s; τ − a(y, s))] − v(p) = 0`;
* (9.3) `∑_{y ∈ N} [f(x, y; τ) − f(y, x; τ − a(y, x))] = 0` for `x ≠ s, t` and `τ = 0, 1, …, p`;
* (9.4) `∑_{τ=0}^{p} ∑_{y ∈ N} [f(t, y; τ) − f(y, t; τ − a(y, t))] + v(p) = 0`;
* (9.5) `0 ≤ f(x, y; τ) ≤ c(x, y)`. -/
def IsDynamicFlow (G : Network N) (s t : N) (p : ℕ) (f : N → N → ℕ → ℝ) (hold : N → ℕ → ℝ)
    (v : ℝ) : Prop :=
  (∑ τ ∈ Finset.range (p + 1), (outAt G f hold s τ - inAt G f hold s τ)) - v = 0 ∧
  (∀ x, x ≠ s → x ≠ t → ∀ τ ≤ p, outAt G f hold x τ - inAt G f hold x τ = 0) ∧
  (∑ τ ∈ Finset.range (p + 1), (outAt G f hold t τ - inAt G f hold t τ)) + v = 0 ∧
  (∀ q ∈ G.arcs, ∀ τ ≤ p, 0 ≤ f q.1 q.2 τ ∧ f q.1 q.2 τ ≤ (G.cap q.1 q.2 : ℝ)) ∧
  (∀ x, ∀ τ ≤ p, 0 ≤ hold x τ)

/-- A maximal dynamic flow for `p` periods (p. 82): a dynamic flow `(f, hold)` with value `v` such
that every dynamic flow from `s` to `t` for `p` periods has value at most `v`. -/
def IsMaximalDynamicFlow (G : Network N) (s t : N) (p : ℕ) (f : N → N → ℕ → ℝ)
    (hold : N → ℕ → ℝ) (v : ℝ) : Prop :=
  IsDynamicFlow G s t p f hold v ∧
    ∀ (f' : N → N → ℕ → ℝ) (hold' : N → ℕ → ℝ) (v' : ℝ),
      IsDynamicFlow G s t p f' hold' v' → v' ≤ v

/-- The set of values of dynamic flows from `s` to `t` for `p` periods; its greatest element is the
maximal dynamic flow value `v̄(p)` (p. 90). -/
def dynamicFlowValues (G : Network N) (s t : N) (p : ℕ) : Set ℝ :=
  {v | ∃ (f : N → N → ℕ → ℝ) (hold : N → ℕ → ℝ), IsDynamicFlow G s t p f hold v}

/-- A chain decomposition of the static flow `f` of value `v` (Chapter I, §2, (2.9)–(2.10) and
Theorem 2.2; Chapter III, (9.8)): finitely many chain flows `(C_r; h_r)`, `r = 1, …, m`, where each
`C_r` is a chain from `s` to `t` and `h_r > 0`, whose superposition stays within `f` on every arc,
`∑_{r : (x, y) ∈ C_r} h_r ≤ f(x, y)`, and whose total value is at least the value of `f`,
`∑_r h_r ≥ v`. -/
def IsChainDecomposition (G : Network N) (s t : N) (f : N → N → ℝ) (v : ℝ) {m : ℕ}
    (C : Fin m → List N) (h : Fin m → ℝ) : Prop :=
  (∀ r, IsChainFromTo G s t (C r)) ∧ (∀ r, 0 < h r) ∧ v ≤ ∑ r, h r ∧
    ∀ q ∈ G.arcs, ∑ r, h r * ((List.zip (C r) (C r).tail).count q : ℝ) ≤ f q.1 q.2

/-- The arcs of the node list `x₁, x₂, …, x_k`, each paired with its departure time when the list is
traversed from time `τ₁ = τ`: the arc `(x_i, x_{i+1})` is entered at `τ_i`, with
`τ_{i+1} = τ_i + a(x_i, x_{i+1})` ((9.9)–(9.10), p. 87). -/
def timedArcs (G : Network N) : List N → ℕ → List ((N × N) × ℕ)
  | x :: y :: rest, τ => ((x, y), τ) :: timedArcs G (y :: rest) (τ + G.cost x y)
  | _, _ => []

/-- The traversal time `σ = ∑_{i=1}^{k−1} a(x_i, x_{i+1})` of the chain `x₁, …, x_k` (p. 86). -/
def traversalTime (G : Network N) (l : List N) : ℕ :=
  (List.zipWith G.cost l l.tail).sum

/-- The temporally repeated flow generated by the chain flows `(C_r; h_r)` for `p` periods
(pp. 84–88): each chain flow is started at every time `θ = 0, 1, …, p` that leaves enough time for
it to arrive at the sink, `θ + σ_r ≤ p`; the copy started at `θ` sends `h_r` along the `i`-th arc of
`C_r` at time `θ + τ_i`. The value at `(x, y; τ)` is the sum of `h_r` over all such copies that use
the arc `(x, y)` at departure time `τ`. No hold-overs are used. -/
def repeatedFlow (G : Network N) (p : ℕ) {m : ℕ} (C : Fin m → List N) (h : Fin m → ℝ) :
    N → N → ℕ → ℝ :=
  fun x y τ => ∑ r, h r * ∑ θ ∈ Finset.range (p + 1),
    if θ + traversalTime G (C r) ≤ p then
      (((timedArcs G (C r) 0).filter (fun e => e.1 = (x, y) ∧ θ + e.2 = τ)).length : ℝ)
    else 0

/-- A dynamic cut for `p` periods: a set `X` of nodes `x(τ)` of the time-expanded network `G(p)`
(pp. 82–83; `x(τ)` is the pair `(x, τ)`, `τ = 0, …, p`) that contains every source `s(τ)` and no
sink `t(τ)`, `τ = 0, …, p`. The cut itself is the set `(X, X̄)` of arcs of `G(p)` leading from `X` to
its complement. Pairs with `τ > p` are not nodes of `G(p)` and are ignored. -/
def IsDynamicCut (s t : N) (p : ℕ) (X : Finset (N × ℕ)) : Prop :=
  ∀ τ ≤ p, (s, τ) ∈ X ∧ (t, τ) ∉ X

/-- Some hold-over arc `[x(τ), x(τ + 1)]`, `0 ≤ τ ≤ p − 1`, of `G(p)` leads from `X` to its
complement. Hold-over arcs have infinite capacity (p. 83), so such a cut has infinite capacity. -/
def HoldoverCrosses (p : ℕ) (X : Finset (N × ℕ)) : Prop :=
  ∃ x τ, τ < p ∧ (x, τ) ∈ X ∧ (x, τ + 1) ∉ X

/-- The capacity of the cut `(X, X̄)` of `G(p)` counting only the replica arcs
`[x(τ), y(τ + a(x, y))]`, `0 ≤ τ ≤ p − a(x, y)`, each of capacity `c(x, y)` (pp. 82–83). It is the
capacity of the cut when no hold-over arc crosses it (`¬ HoldoverCrosses p X`). -/
def dynamicCutCapacity (G : Network N) (p : ℕ) (X : Finset (N × ℕ)) : ℝ :=
  ∑ q ∈ G.arcs, ∑ τ ∈ Finset.range (p + 1),
    if τ + G.cost q.1 q.2 ≤ p ∧ (q.1, τ) ∈ X ∧ (q.2, τ + G.cost q.1 q.2) ∉ X then
      (G.cap q.1 q.2 : ℝ)
    else 0

/-- A minimal dynamic cut for `p` periods: a minimal cut in `G(p)` separating the sources `s(τ)`
from the sinks `t(τ)`. It is a dynamic cut of finite capacity (no hold-over arc crosses it) whose
capacity is at most that of every other dynamic cut of finite capacity; a dynamic cut crossed by a
hold-over arc has infinite capacity and so never undercuts it. -/
def IsMinimalDynamicCut (G : Network N) (s t : N) (p : ℕ) (X : Finset (N × ℕ)) : Prop :=
  IsDynamicCut s t p X ∧ ¬ HoldoverCrosses p X ∧
    ∀ Y : Finset (N × ℕ), IsDynamicCut s t p Y → ¬ HoldoverCrosses p Y →
      dynamicCutCapacity G p X ≤ dynamicCutCapacity G p Y

/-- The node set (9.18) of `G(p)` determined by integer node numbers `π`:
`X = {x(τ) | π(x) ≤ τ}`, `τ = 0, …, p`. -/
def nodeNumberCut (π : N → ℤ) (p : ℕ) : Finset (N × ℕ) :=
  (Finset.univ ×ˢ Finset.range (p + 1)).filter (fun q => π q.1 ≤ (q.2 : ℤ))

end FlowsNetworks.DynamicFlow


